      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 3670.9,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 701,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two types of atoms: hydrogen and oxygen. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**—meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it is so important:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater is famous for being utterly normal in some ways and profoundly weird in others:\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (water), **solid** (ice), and **gas** (steam or water vapor).\n* **No Color, Taste, or Smell:** Pure water is transparent, odorless, and tasteless, though we often taste the minerals dissolved inside it.\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals) than any other liquid. This is why it is essential for transporting nutrients in living things.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Almost all liquids shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice floats on water. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, making life on Earth impossible.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat with only small changes in its own temperature. This helps regulate the climate of the Earth and keeps living organisms from overheating.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nWater is the defining feature of our home planet, often called the \"Blue Planet.\"\n* **Abundance:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered in water, mostly by oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** Despite how much water there is, **only about 2.5% is fresh water**. Even worse, over two-thirds of that fresh water is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use in lakes, rivers, and the atmosphere.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water on Earth is constantly moving in a closed loop driven by the sun. It evaporates into the air, condenses into clouds, falls back to the ground as precipitation (rain, snow), and flows into rivers and oceans to start all over again.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Importance to Life\nAs far as science knows, **life cannot exist without water.** \n* **Biological Solvent:** Inside living things, water acts as the medium where all the chemical reactions of life take place. \n* **Human Body:** The human body is made up of roughly 60% water. It uses water to regulate body temperature, flush out waste, lubricate joints, and carry oxygen and nutrients to cells through the blood.\n* **Ecosystems:** Water shapes landscapes through erosion and provides habitats for millions of aquatic species, ranging from microscopic bacteria to the blue whale.\n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties that make it the absolute bedrock of chemistry, climate, and life itself."
